ADJKERNTZ(8)                System Manager's Manual               ADJKERNTZ(8)

NNAAMMEE
     aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz - adjust local time CMOS clock to reflect time zone changes and
     keep current timezone offset for the kernel

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz --ii
     aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz --aa [--ss]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz maintains the proper relationship between the kernel clock,
     which is always set to UTC, and the CMOS clock, which may be set to local
     time.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz also informs the kernel about machine timezone shifts to
     maintain proper timestamps for local time file systems such as the MS-DOS
     file system. The main purpose of this thing is not general fixing of
     initially broken MS-DOS file timestamp idea but keeping the same
     timestamps between FreeBSD MS-DOS file system and MS-DOS operating system
     installed on the same machine.  If the file _/_e_t_c_/_w_a_l_l___c_m_o_s___c_l_o_c_k exists,
     it means that CMOS clock keeps local time (MS-DOS and MS-Windows
     compatible mode).  If that file does not exist, it means that the CMOS
     clock keeps UTC time.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz passes this state to the
     _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._w_a_l_l___c_m_o_s___c_l_o_c_k kernel variable.

     Adjustments may be needed at system startup and shutdown, and whenever a
     time zone change occurs.  To handle these different situations, aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz
     is invoked in two ways:

     --ii    This form handles system startups and shutdowns.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz is
           invoked with this option from _/_e_t_c_/_r_c on entry to multi-user mode,
           before any other daemons have been started.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz puts itself
           into the background.  Then, for a local time CMOS clock, aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz
           reads the local time from it and sets the kernel clock to the
           corresponding UTC time.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz also stores the local time zone
           offset into the _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z kernel variable, for use by
           subsequent invocations of _'_a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z _-_a_' and by local time file
           systems.

           For a local time CMOS clock _'_a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z _-_i_' pauses, and remains
           inactive as a background daemon until it receives a SIGTERM.  The
           SIGTERM will normally be sent by init(8) when the system leaves
           multi-user mode (usually, because the system is being shut down).
           After receiving the SIGTERM, aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz reads the UTC kernel clock
           and updates the CMOS clock, if necessary, to ensure that it
           reflects the current local time zone.  Then aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz exits.

     --aa [--ss]
           This form is used to update the local time CMOS clock and kernel
           _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z variable when time zone changes occur, e.g., when
           entering or leaving daylight savings time.  AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz uses the
           kernel clock's UTC time, the previously stored time zone offset,
           and the changed time zone rule to calculate a new time zone offset.
           It stores the new offset into the _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z kernel
           variable, and updates the wall CMOS clock to the new local time.
           If _'_a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z _-_a_' was started at a nonexistent time (during a
           timezone change), it exits with a warning diagnostic unless the --ss
           option was used, in which case aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz sleeps 30 minutes and
           tries again.

           This form should be invoked from root's crontab(5) every half hour
           between midnight and 5am, when most modern time zone changes occur.
           Warning: don't use the --ss option in a crontab(5) command line, or
           multiple _'_a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z _-_a_' instances could conflict with each other.

     AAddjjkkeerrnnttzz clears the kernel timezone structure and makes the kernel clock
     run in the UTC time zone.  Super-user privileges are required for all
     operations.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     TZ          Time zone change rule, see tzset(3); not needed when
                 tzsetup(8) or zic(8) is used.

FFIILLEESS
     _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_c_a_l_t_i_m_e        Current zoneinfo file, see tzsetup(8) and zic(8).
     _/_e_t_c_/_w_a_l_l___c_m_o_s___c_l_o_c_k  Empty file.  Its presence indicates that the
                           machine's CMOS clock is set to local time, while
                           its absence indicates a UTC CMOS clock.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     tzset(3), crontab(5), mount_msdos(8), rc(8), sysctl(8), tzsetup(8),
     zic(8)

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     No diagnostics.  If an error occurs, aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz logs an error message via
     syslog(3) and exits with a nonzero return code.

BBUUGGSS
     On FGX, `adjkerntz -i' exits once it has adjusted the kernel at startup.
     It does not remain as a background daemon waiting for SIGTERM, which
     would hold a process slot and memory for the whole uptime; a time zone
     change the machine is up across is left to `adjkerntz -a' from
     crontab(5), as above.

     _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z is a _l_o_n_g on FGX, where _i_n_t is 16 bits and could not
     hold a zone more than nine hours from UTC.

     FGX does not write the IIgs clock unless machdep.disable_rtc_set is
     cleared: it is 1 by default, where FreeBSD's is 0, because the clock
     belongs to the machine and GS/OS reads it too.  So `adjkerntz -a' at a
     daylight saving change moves the kernel's offset and leaves the IIgs
     clock as it was; set the IIgs clock to the new local time, as GS/OS
     needs anyway, or FGX reads it an hour out at the next boot.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Andrey A. Chernov <ache@astral.msk.su>

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The aaddjjkkeerrnnttzz command appeared in FreeBSD 1.0.

